  "theory": {
        "intro": "The factor theorem is the remainder theorem with a zero remainder: the divisor fits exactly. It lets you test factors by substitution, then divide to factorise cubics and quartics completely and solve $P(x)=0$.",
        "facts": [
          "**Factor theorem:** $(x-a)$ is a factor of $P(x)$ if $P(a)=0$.",
          "The value $a$ is then called a **zero** of the polynomial.",
          "In general, $(ax-b)$ is a factor of $P(x)$ if $P\\left(\\frac{b}{a}\\right)=0$.",
          "**Integer root theorem:** if $P(x)$ has integer coefficients, every integer root is a factor of the constant term, so test those first.",
          "A polynomial of degree $n$ has **at most $n$ zeroes**.",
          "The remainder when a polynomial is divided by one of its factors is $0$.",
          "Once one factor $(x-a)$ is found, divide to get $P(x)=(x-a)Q(x)$, then factorise the quotient $Q(x)$.",
          "To solve $P(x)=0$, factorise fully and apply the **null factor law**: a product is zero when one factor is zero."
        ],
        "steps": [
          "Find a factor",
          "Divide",
          "Factorise the quotient",
          "Solve"
        ]
      },
