        "solution_text": "1. **Find a factor**\n$P(1)=1-2-5+6$\n$=0$\n$P(1)=0$, so $(x-1)$ is a factor.\n2. **Divide**\n$x^3\\div x=x^2$\n$(x^3-2x^2-5x+6)-x^2(x-1)$\n$=(x^3-2x^2-5x+6)-(x^3-x^2)$\n$=-x^2-5x+6$\n$-x^2\\div x=-x$\n$(-x^2-5x+6)-(-x)(x-1)$\n$=(-x^2-5x+6)-(-x^2+x)$\n$=-6x+6$\n$-6x\\div x=-6$\n$(-6x+6)-(-6)(x-1)$\n$=(-6x+6)-(-6x+6)$\n$=0$\n$\\therefore P(x)=(x-1)(x^2-x-6)$\n3. **Factorise the quotient**\n$P(x)=(x-1)(x-3)(x+2)$"
